 ],
 "literal": "Eastern sea wave's flight-lost heron / strong-charioted Poṟaiyaṉ's Toṇṭi front-shore / ayirai fish abundant food to approach like / she who is far away, difficult to attain, you think of / you have pain, O heart, you who are of the nature of pain!",
 "translation": "O heart, like the heron that has lost its strength of flight in the waves of the eastern sea and approaches the front shore of Tondi, belonging to Poraiyan of the strong chariot, to feed on the abundant ayirai fish, you think of her who is far away and difficult to attain. You are in pain, O heart, you whose very nature is pain!",
 "modern": "கிழக்குக் கடலின் அலைகளால் பறக்கும் திறனை இழந்த நாரையானது, வலிமையான தேரை உடைய பொறையனது தொண்டித் துறையில் உள்ள அயிரை மீனாகிய சிறந்த இரையை நாடிச் செல்வது போல, தொலைவில் உள்ளவளும் அடைவதற்கு அரியவளுமாகிய தலைவியைப் பற்றி நீ நினைக்கின்றாய். நெஞ்சமே! நீ துன்பப்படுகிறாய்; துன்பத்தையே இயல்பாகக் கொண்டவன் நீ!",
 "explanation": "In this poem, the hero speaks to his own heart, expressing his deep longing and helplessness. He has fallen in love with the heroine, who is physically distant and difficult to reach. He compares his heart's yearning to a weak, flight-weary heron that has lost its strength in the rough waves of the eastern sea and can only crawl or struggle toward the shore of Tondi to feed on the small ayirai fish. This imagery belongs to the Neytal (coastal) landscape, which traditionally represents the theme of longing, anxiety, and despair (iraṅkal). The key words are 'குணகடல்' (eastern sea), 'நாரை' (heron), 'தொண்டி' (Tondi), and 'நோய்' (pain/sickness).",
